#---
# purpose: "a resource a function made and dropped runs its destructor exactly once, whether the collection that frees it happens mid-run or never happens before exit: the interpreter, JIT and AOT print the same single line"
#---
#
# `drop_one` makes a resource and returns without keeping it. `main` then builds
# 20000 list cells and checks their count without printing, so the only output
# is the destructor's line. On the JIT and AOT paths a collection during the
# allocation finds the resource dead and runs its destructor at that boundary;
# the interpreter, which does not collect, runs it at exit. Either way the line
# appears once.
extern "C" defp fz_resource_test_print_dtor(integer) :: nil
def dwrap(x), do: fz_resource_test_print_dtor(x)
def drop_one() do
  _r = make_resource(77, &dwrap/1)
  :dropped
end
def churn(0, acc), do: acc
def churn(n, acc), do: churn(n - 1, [Integer.to_string(n) | acc])
def main() do
  drop_one()
  20000 = length(churn(20000, []))
  :done
end
